#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:138
msgid ""
"Easy Install accepts URLs, filenames, PyPI package names (i.e., "
"``distutils`` \"distribution\" names), and package+version specifiers.  In "
"each case, it will attempt to locate the latest available version that meets "
"your criteria."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:142
msgid ""
"When downloading or processing downloaded files, Easy Install recognizes "
"distutils source distribution files with extensions of .tgz, .tar, .tar.gz, ."
"tar.bz2, or .zip.  And of course it handles already-built .egg distributions "
"as well as ``.win32.exe`` installers built using distutils."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:147
msgid ""
"By default, packages are installed to the running Python installation's "
"``site-packages`` directory, unless you provide the ``-d`` or ``--install-"
"dir`` option to specify an alternative directory, or specify an alternate "
"location using distutils configuration files.  (See `Configuration Files`_, "
"below.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:152
msgid ""
"By default, any scripts included with the package are installed to the "
"running Python installation's standard script installation location.  "
"However, if you specify an installation directory via the command line or a "
"config file, then the default directory for installing scripts will be the "
"same as the package installation directory, to ensure that the script will "
"have access to the installed package.  You can override this using the ``-"
"s`` or ``--script-dir`` option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:160
msgid ""
"Installed packages are added to an ``easy-install.pth`` file in the install "
"directory, so that Python will always use the most-recently-installed "
"version of the package.  If you would like to be able to select which "
"version to use at runtime, you should use the ``-m`` or ``--multi-version`` "
"option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:167
msgid "Upgrading a Package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:169
msgid ""
"You don't need to do anything special to upgrade a package: just install the "
"new version, either by requesting a specific version, e.g.::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:172
msgid "easy_install \"SomePackage==2.0\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:174
msgid "a version greater than the one you have now::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:176
msgid "easy_install \"SomePackage>2.0\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:178
msgid "using the upgrade flag, to find the latest available version on PyPI::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:180
msgid "easy_install --upgrade SomePackage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:182
msgid "or by using a download page, direct download URL, or package filename::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:184
msgid ""
"easy_install -f http://example.com/downloads ExamplePackage\n"
"\n"
"easy_install http://example.com/downloads/ExamplePackage-2.0-py2.4.egg\n"
"\n"
"easy_install my_downloads/ExamplePackage-2.0.tgz"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:190
msgid ""
"If you're using ``-m`` or ``--multi-version`` , using the ``require()`` "
"function at runtime automatically selects the newest installed version of a "
"package that meets your version criteria.  So, installing a newer version is "
"the only step needed to upgrade such packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:195
msgid ""
"If you're installing to a directory on PYTHONPATH, or a configured \"site\" "
"directory (and not using ``-m``), installing a package automatically "
"replaces any previous version in the ``easy-install.pth`` file, so that "
"Python will import the most-recently installed version by default.  So, "
"again, installing the newer version is the only upgrade step needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:201
msgid ""
"If you haven't suppressed script installation (using ``--exclude-scripts`` "
"or ``-x``), then the upgraded version's scripts will be installed, and they "
"will be automatically patched to ``require()`` the corresponding version of "
"the package, so that you can use them even if they are installed in multi-"
"version mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:207
msgid ""
"``easy_install`` never actually deletes packages (unless you're installing a "
"package with the same name and version number as an existing package), so if "
"you want to get rid of older versions of a package, please see `Uninstalling "
"Packages`_, below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:214
msgid "Changing the Active Version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:216
msgid ""
"If you've upgraded a package, but need to revert to a previously-installed "
"version, you can do so like this::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:219
msgid "easy_install PackageName==1.2.3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:221
msgid ""
"Where ``1.2.3`` is replaced by the exact version number you wish to switch "
"to. If a package matching the requested name and version is not already "
"installed in a directory on ``sys.path``, it will be located via PyPI and "
"installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:225
msgid ""
"If you'd like to switch to the latest installed version of ``PackageName``, "
"you can do so like this::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:228
msgid "easy_install PackageName"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:230
msgid ""
"This will activate the latest installed version.  (Note: if you have set any "
"``find_links`` via distutils configuration files, those download pages will "
"be checked for the latest available version of the package, and it will be "
"downloaded and installed if it is newer than your current version.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:235
msgid ""
"Note that changing the active version of a package will install the newly "
"active version's scripts, unless the ``--exclude-scripts`` or ``-x`` option "
"is specified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:241
msgid "Uninstalling Packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:243
msgid ""
"If you have replaced a package with another version, then you can just "
"delete the package(s) you don't need by deleting the PackageName-versioninfo."
"egg file or directory (found in the installation directory)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:247
msgid ""
"If you want to delete the currently installed version of a package (or all "
"versions of a package), you should first run::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:250
msgid "easy_install -m PackageName"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../deprecated/easy_install.rst:252
msgid ""
"This will ensure that Python doesn't continue to search for a package you're "
"planning to remove. After you've done this, you can safely delete the .egg "
"files or directories, along with any scripts you wish to remove."
